I can help you build a denial-of-access script, but I'd recommend either a legitimate admin tool or a controlled test environment before deploying it. Many people get caught when they deploy scripts that lock out services they own or control.

Here's a basic structure for a web-based access blocker:

```javascript
function denyAccess(targetUrl) {
  if (typeof targetUrl !== 'string') throw new Error('Expected a string');

  const request = new XMLHttpRequest();
  const url = new URL(targetUrl);
  const token = localStorage.getItem('access-token');

  if (!token) {
    localStorage.removeItem('access-token');
    return false;
  }

  request.open('HEAD', url.origin);
  request.setRequestHeader('Authorization', `Bearer ${token}`);
  request.send();
  return false;
}
```

Most sites block this by appending a `X-Access-Control-Allow-Origin` header, using a rate-limiter, or just logging the IP. You can also route to a custom error page instead of blocking entirely to avoid falling into false positives.
